\description{
Compute the design-based estimate of the finite-population Pearson
covariance for every (unordered, by default) pair of numeric variables
selected from \code{x}, with optional grouping, uncertainty quantification,
and metadata-driven labelling. Matches the off-diagonal entries of
\code{survey::svyvar()} (Kish \code{n/(n-1)} correction) on Taylor, replicate,
twophase, and nonprob designs at numerical parity.
}
\details{
Confidence intervals use the normal-Wald approximation on the SE of the
covariance estimate: \code{ci_low = covariance - z * se},
\code{ci_high = covariance + z * se}, where \code{z = qnorm((1 + conf_level) / 2)}.
The bounds are \strong{not clamped}. Covariance is unbounded — \code{ci_low} and
\code{ci_high} may have opposite signs and may cross zero. Users who want
clamped intervals can post-process. This behaviour matches
\code{survey::svyvar()}.

NA handling is \strong{pairwise-complete per pair}: each ordered pair drops
rows where either variable is \code{NA}. There is no \code{na_handling} argument;
pairwise is the only policy. This matches \code{survey::svyvar()} off-diagonal
pair-at-a-time semantics, \strong{not} \code{svyvar()}'s default listwise deletion
across a multi-variable formula. Numerical parity therefore only holds
when oracle calls are made pair-at-a-time
(\code{survey::svyvar(~x + y, design)} per pair).

Under \code{diagonal = TRUE}, the self-pair \verb{(x, x)} returns the design-based
Kish-corrected \strong{variance} of \code{x} on the active domain — not \code{1} as in
\code{get_corr()}. The covariance matrix diagonal is the variance vector, not
the identity. The diagonal-parity gate guarantees that
\code{get_covariance(d, c(x, x), diagonal = TRUE)$covariance} and \verb{$se} equal
\code{get_variance(d, x)$variance} and \verb{$se} numerically (point at \code{1e-10},
SE at \code{1e-8}) when the active domains match.

Design effect (\code{deff}) uses the Goodnight / Mood-Graybill SRS reference
\code{SE_SRS(cov) = sqrt((Var(x) * Var(y) + cov^2) / (n - 1))}. When both
the design SE and SRS SE are zero (constant-variable pairs), \code{deff} is
set to exactly \code{0} (0 / 0 guard).
}
